1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:25,400
HOST: High in the Andes sits a mountain the Spanish called Cerro Rico, the Rich Hill. In 1545 they struck silver there, and the city below it would swell to two hundred thousand people, as large as London.  The workers called it something else: the mountain that eats men. How did one mountain rewire the world's economy, and what did it cost?

2
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:40,799
HOST: By the end, three answers. One: how the mita draft fed workers to Potosí's mines. Two: how American silver caused the price revolution. Three: silver's two streams. Potosí's silver east to Europe; Mexico's west to Manila.

3
00:00:40,799 --> 00:01:04,599
HOST: The story goes that in 1545, an Andean herder named Diego Huallpa chased lost llamas. He built a fire, and the ground beneath it melted into shining liquid. Silver.  Within years, Potosí was producing most of the world's silver. But Spain had a problem. The mountain was rich, and there was almost no one to dig it.

4
00:01:04,599 --> 00:01:24,000
HOST: Spain's answer was the mita. The Inca had used a labor draft called the mit'a for roads and terraces, taking turns. In 1573, the viceroy Francisco de Toledo repurposed it for the mines.  Here is the order. Then listen to a man who lived under it.

5
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:38,599
DECREE: Each village shall send one man in seven to labor in the mines of Potosí for one year. They shall receive a wage. They shall return to their homes when the term is done.

6
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:57,199
MITAYO: They took us from our villages to the cold mountain. We climbed down on rope ladders, candles tied to our thumbs. We crushed ore with our hands. We breathed the mercury smoke.  The wage could not buy back our health. Many never came home.

7
00:01:57,199 --> 00:02:21,000
HOST: The silver came out through poison. Miners mixed crushed ore with mercury on open patios and trod it barefoot. The mercury grabbed the silver; heat burned it away as vapor. It worked on low-grade ore the old smelters could not touch, which is why Potosí kept producing for two centuries. It also poisoned everyone who touched it.

8
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:39,199
HOST: Silver crossed the Atlantic to Spain. More money chasing the same goods meant prices climbed across Europe. By 1600, prices were three to four times higher than in 1500, the first great inflation of the modern world. Historians call it the price revolution.

9
00:02:39,199 --> 00:03:01,000
HOST: Spain became the richest country in Europe, its silver fleets returning every year. So Spain won?  Not so fast. Spain spent the silver on wars and imports instead of industry. By the end of the 1500s, the richest empire on earth had gone bankrupt four times. The silver made Spain's rivals richer.

10
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:28,400
HOST: Follow the silver, in two streams. Potosí's silver crossed the Atlantic to Spain. And from 1565, Mexican silver sailed west: the Manila galleons carried it across the Pacific, from Acapulco to Manila, trading it for silk and porcelain. China hungered for silver for its tax system; as much as a third of all American silver ended up in Asia. One mountain, wired to the whole world.

11
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:38,400
